@@ -1,5 +1,13 @@
 # AdrenoMetal
 
+GPU052 carries upstream fixes 56ce432 and 6d4a3f3: texture descriptors and views
+preserve `allowGPUOptimizedContents`, and absent library functions report
+`MTLLibraryErrorFunctionNotFound` so a caller can continue its library search.
+The iPhone/A840 adaptations, GPU051 foreground allocation sizing, and existing
+multi-level transfer paths remain in place.
+
+Metal frontend through metal2vulkan and Turnip.
+
 Metal frontend through `UniXNU/metal2vulkan` and Turnip.
 
 ## Translator and runtime
@@ -8,22 +16,336 @@ This repository targets the `UniXNU/metal2vulkan` fork (`development` branch, pi
 
 The five UniXNU runtime and translator changes are maintained directly in `UniXNU/metal2vulkan` rather than as carried files under `bridge/patches/`:
 
+## Device limits
+
+Direct readers of the original Metal device's limits receive the same measured
+values as the frontend getters. The field locations and widths are discovered
+through the original getters, following upstream a38e12a. On iPhone24A446,
+IconRendering reads the maximum 2D width and height directly from this table;
+an empty table rejects nonempty icon textures. The memory-pressure event source
+also retains process lifetime under ARC, following upstream46fdc91, so its
+existing resource-release handler remains available after initialization.
+Actual icon latency and Core Image program creation remain hardware checks.
+
+Core Image's precompiled-kernel lookup on iPhone24A446 calls
+`newExternFunctionWithName:`. The library forwards it to the existing function
+lookup, retaining the same returned-function ownership and the library's
+stitching bitcode. Missing names remain nil. This also accepts ordinary functions
+as the runtime's corresponding library method does.
+
+Function-constant objects expose both `required` and `isRequired`. Both read
+the same runtime-supplied flag. Original24A446 Core Image calls `required`;
+providing only `isRequired` caused the camera service to terminate during
+program setup. The selector consumer test checks true/false changes through
+the public Metal type and native Objective-C message without creating a GPU.
+
+In a diagnostic boot, the existing icon-service trace also records the first
+96 library loads, function-name queries and external-kernel lookups. File-read
+failure is recorded before it returns nil, and stitching requests report their
+graph and function counts. These records distinguish program setup from the
+already recorded device, queue and completed work; they do not change the calls'
+results.
+
+Stitching identifies functions by the SHA-256 of their retained AIR module,
+so functions from the same module return the same `bitCodeHash`. A stitching
+request carries each distinct module once, with its original `bhas` tag,
+while retaining the complete function graph. This avoids sending duplicate
+module definitions to the system compiler. The compiler consumer checks shared
+modules, distinct modules and repeated function objects without opening a GPU;
+physical frame latency still requires device verification.
+
+On iPhone 24A446, App Library icon-view lookup uses the existing diffable data
+source's item index when the indexed identifier, current query and displayed
+icon agree. This removes repeated localized collation and array searches from
+visible-icon enumeration. Missing items, updates and recycled cells retain the
+original lookup. Installation checks the original SpringBoardHome and UIKit
+image UUIDs and method signatures; other OS builds use their existing methods.
+
+## Memory reuse
+
 - Nonpresenting Vulkan instance creation without surface or swapchain requirements for native scanout.
 - Deferred native display scheduling after the final driver submission so display fence capture includes the current frame.
 - Read-only host-memory buffer imports.
 - Zero-copy in-place linear textures (with `VK_EXT_attachment_feedback_loop_layout`) over imported host memory.
 - Entry-return floor tail guard across translator shader stages.
 
+Unused owned image backing can serve later pooled or dedicated images. Image spares expire after ten seconds and are reclaimed on allocation failure or memory pressure; they have no byte limit. Unused buffer reservations share a separate 64 MiB device budget across memory types. The room worker rechecks demand and reuses a spare of the same pool and memory type before allocating. If foreground completions restore room during allocation, the new owned buffer stays spare with its address object intact. Owned blocks are freed on the runtime's existing free thread; a full 64-item queue releases on the caller. Allocation failure reclaims spares and waits for posted frees before retrying. Imported memory and owner callbacks retain their release ordering.
+
+Pooled allocations can reuse a larger spare when the current request would create a smaller
+block. The remainder stays available in the pool. Dedicated allocations retain their original
+fit rule and new buffer contents are still cleared.
+
+With the existing GPU diagnostic switch enabled, commands taking at least 100 ms from creation
+through application completion handlers and synchronous Metal API calls taking at least 16 ms
+also record their original time boundaries in the existing USB problem channel. Gaps of at
+least 100 ms between a rendering thread's previous commit and its next command creation are
+recorded separately; they may include idle time. Boot UUID, timebase and actual OS memory size
+identify the records. These timings distinguish pre-submit work and notification delay from
+GPU execution and do not measure panel FPS.
+
+System compiler requests recheck the existing reply cache after waiting for the
+compiler mutex. Successful replies are published before releasing that mutex,
+so simultaneous requests for the same compiler, type and bytes reuse the first
+reply. Compiler failures remain uncached.
+
+The read-only display observer checks the original internal and external display
+address points as well as the generic and wireless classes. It prefers a display
+with a verified timer, and then the internal display among equally complete
+readers. The bounded discovery and original memory/UUID checks remain in place;
+display selection changes no system display or timer state.
+An object without a timer or vsync source remains a readable candidate while
+discovery continues; its presence alone does not mark timing discovery ready.
+
+For iPhone 24A446 diagnostics, that same observer reads the original render
+Context registry, deferred-head time and dependency count, local deferral count,
+and linked-context pointer. Its one-millisecond sampling budget and repeated
+field checks report incomplete or changing reads explicitly. Context IDs let
+the retained messages and physical frame holds be compared to these states.
+
+The runtime keeps command counts in each open encoder and reuses unchanged resource-residency facts. Merge scratch can be lent to later encoders of the same command buffer, with its original barriers and leases. A shadow may remain current across command buffers only for tracked private memory owned by the runtime; heaps and external memory retain their required copies. `sheng-metal-flow=1` separately enables lowering, direct commit submission and pipeline warming for experiments; it defaults to off, and pipeline waits remain exact.
+
+## Heap texture copies
+
+Fixed runtime-owned Private heap textures can write complete texel words directly
+to their heap buffer when the descriptor range is aligned. Tracked heaps can also
+read it directly. Untracked heaps retain their input snapshot and staging keep;
+their concurrent-writer merges use the original scratch path. Shared or imported
+memory, special surfaces and ineligible layouts retain staging. Staged output
+preserves only untouched edge ranges when that saves at least half a full copy.
+The original barriers and alias rules remain in force. CPU Vulkan checks cover
+production ShadowCopy dispatch, access stages, padding and independent writes;
+A840 frame performance requires a hardware capture.
+
+Internal transfer buffers use Vulkan's preferred copy offset and row alignment.
+The conversion kernel carries the actual transfer pitch, while Metal-visible
+heap offsets, padding and alias contents retain their layout. This lets Turnip
+record a two-dimensional image copy instead of splitting unaligned rows into
+individual blits. Vulkan operation records retain the recording thread identity
+for correlation with encoder timings. Image-to-buffer records also carry the
+region count; a single-region record includes its buffer row length and the
+low 32 bits of its buffer offset, so row and offset alignment can be checked.
+These timestamps are CPU recording boundaries, not GPU copy durations.
+The existing type 11 copy-row counters are retained when they change, sampled
+by the frame writer every 250 ms, to report actual per-row blits separately.
+
+Single-sample tiled mip chains with 4-, 8- or 16-byte elements use the existing
+tile-coordinate kernel in both directions, with a distinct shape and descriptor
+for each level. Input leaves transfer padding unchanged, as the generic wide-word
+path does. The image transfers, staging, heap ownership, barriers and independent
+writer merges retain their existing behavior. Narrow elements and unsupported
+dispatch grids keep the generic conversion.
+
+Linear heap shadows on Turnip keep their Metal-visible tight rows. For single-sample,
+single-level, single-layer 2D color images with 4, 8 or 16 bytes per texel and a
+row pitch that misses 64-byte alignment, the existing unsigned-integer view and
+compute copy write the original bits back in place. Unsupported views or dispatch
+ranges retain the copy path. Region bounds, independent-writer merges and image
+view ownership follow the existing shadow implementation. CPU Vulkan tests cover
+the remaining 1202 by 2610 BGRA shape, sRGB, wider words and copy fallbacks;
+performance on A840 awaits the candidate's hardware capture.
+
+The diagnostic operation budget is 2,097,152 records (144 MiB plus one exhaustion
+record), covering a longer sequence of glass and application animation tests.
+After exhaustion, frame, CPU-stage and copy-row summaries continue. The operation
+hook and its bound remain disabled when diagnostics are disabled.
+
 ## Bridge architecture
 
 Indirect buffer and texture declarations, heap declarations, encoder barriers, concurrent compute and queue enqueueing use the runtime public APIs. Commands retain both Objective-C resource owners and consumer placement leases until real GPU completion. Native IOSurface writes are marked across render attachments, resolves, blits and shader-write bindings; ordinary commands keep upstream scheduling behavior.
 
+The existing thread-sample records use version 2: up to sixteen frame-pointer
+entries and raw ARM64 x0–x3/x19–x28 from the first `thread_get_state` result.
+This identifies allocation and image-preparation arguments at a sampled call
+without another register query or dereferencing those arguments. The later
+check still compares only PC/LR/FP/SP. Image-catalog records remain version 1;
+readers retain support for the earlier 336-byte thread samples. A 30-minute UI
+capture is bounded below 80 MiB, including its image catalog.
+
+The iPhone24A446 build profile excludes iPad-specific image-relative QuartzCore
+reads/writes and instruction patches, retaining public environment policy only.
+An optional `NativeAdrenoMetal.fulltest` marker next to a published bundle runs
+the maintained raw/Turnip/bridge/frontend checks once in backboardd on a separate
+worker while normal discovery and presentation continue. It does not create a
+second scanout server. Individual T/M/F verdicts remain authoritative; a worker
+return is not a blanket pass. Fatal GPU/transport prerequisites still stop their
+dependent submissions. Completed text lines carry the existing wire's optional
+end marker so the kernel/USB log need not sample only the last cached row.
+
+The integrated checks reuse the published runtime's already writable cache
+directory, and drop their extra frontend-device reference after completion.
+They may create a second Vulkan instance/device while the published device is
+alive: the Darwin KMD must supply independent connection/VA/fence ownership.
+A process-global connection closed by enumeration or another device's teardown
+does not satisfy this contract. The iPhone A840 trial verified raw compute and
+triangle pixels, then exposed that lifetime conflict; complete Metal/UI
+acceptance remains separate from these shader results.
+
+The integrated worker also reuses upstream243d63c GMEM/system-memory colour
+checks on device and imported host images. The local integration declares the
+copy destination usage, performs the initial image layout transition, drains
+submitted work before teardown, and retains resources when quiescence is unknown.
+Every pixel is checked against the fixture, with setup errors kept separate.
+Bridge counters logged before cleanup are labelled `M-body cleanup-pending`;
+only the later M/F verdict records completed cleanup and frontend results.
+
+Upstream6416a76 icon-service notifications are retained in the existing event65
+channel: device and queue creation, and at most eight completed draw/dispatch
+commands. These observations do not change the iPhone process's launchd policy.
+The sheng-only Core Image environment choice is not assumed valid for iPhone27.
+
+With `sheng-gpu-trace=1` the render server writes its frame trace (command phases, encoding batches, memory decisions, completion stages, attachments and Turnip pass and submission records) to its own file. Only event 63, the render, encode and completion thread identities, reaches the kernel ring. See [GPU frame diagnostics](https://unixnu.com/porting/gpu-diagnostics) for fields and decoding.
+
 AdrenoMetal owns descriptor-index grants, heap and buffer placement leases, bounded completion waits, pooled private storage for memoryless descriptors, and compute-based two-plane YCbCr IOSurface conversion with seed-tracked refresh. Commands retain consumer ownership tokens through GPU retirement. Texture views retain backing leases separately from descriptor indices.
 
 Binary archives and the read-only system cache populate the runtime cache directory, isolated by runtime build identity. Imports preserve the generation lock, byte accounting and version record. A system-cache writer publishes readable files; ordinary applications create no publication worker. Pipeline creation and cache decoding remain runtime operations.
 
+`sheng-metal-flow=1` enables the runtime's lowering thread, commit submission and pipeline warm pass for testing. The switch defaults to off. Either value of `sheng-metal-flow=` logs the `FL` counts to os_log when they change.
+
+Slow encoding operations retain source lines and enqueue times. Completion handlers and resource destructors carry individual timing and identities. Kernel sampling of the render, encode and completion threads and blocked-thread waits provide the cross-thread wait record.
+
 ## Validation
 
+`python3 scripts/check-wallpaper-freeze-host.py` in the resolved tree checks real pixels, callbacks, concurrent parked submissions and process selection.
+
+
+The local fixed-private-heap path can copy a single-level, single-layer 2D image
+back through an image-owned unsigned view directly into Metal's tiled bytes.
+It reuses the existing Morton coordinate kernel and writes consecutive words;
+the original tight transfer buffer is still used for copy-in and keep/merge
+output. The sampled image read and fixed heap write are tracked as compute uses.
+Unsupported views, shapes, mapped/imported heaps and changed buffer identities
+retain the existing transfer. This adds no driver performance fact.
+
+GPU026's local qualification executes40 raw-view/padding cases,18 private heap
+fused copies,27 fallback cases,18 independent-writer merges and12 actual image
+owner/range-reuse cases, within117 affected runtime tests. Four generated image
+copy modules pass SPIR-V validation; the original nine tiled translator tests,
+Clippy and Rustdoc pass. A840 timing, mutable-view mode and rendering time still
+require hardware comparison; these CPU-device results are not a frame-rate claim.
+
+
+The diagnostic pass records can retain driver-call entry uptime and wall time
+for BeginRenderPass and EndRenderPass in their previously empty detail words.
+They keep the existing command/thread identity,72-byte format and event budget;
+there are no extra records or clock reads without the installed diagnostic hook.
+These clocks include scheduling and waits and do not measure GPU or pure CPU
+execution. The real recorder callback test checks unchanged arguments, exactly
+one call/record, disabled-hook passthrough and the recorded time bounds.
+
+
+Automatic shader priming keeps waiting while the foreground commit-rate gate
+is busy; elapsed time alone no longer starts a compile. The existing two quiet
+seconds still release a busy wait. Function preparation and periodic directory
+scan checkpoints share that gate. Cache identity, completed-function records,
+shader inputs, background QoS and the initial90-second delay remain unchanged.
+The wait function is checked with controlled commit/clock schedules, including
+the previous10-second busy release; foreground performance needs hardware data.
+
+
+The diagnostic driver phase hook writes bounded slow-call groups into the existing
+frame trace. Groups identify the Vulkan call, thread, inclusive and exclusive
+phase wall times and the call's thread CPU time. Only calls of at least8ms are
+retained, up to4096 groups of33 records (72bytes each). Incomplete groups remain
+unknown. CPU query overhead stays in the measured interval; non-running wall
+time includes both waits and descheduling. No new USB protocol or Native ABI.
+
+The local Darwin KMD retains one recording connection and caps each fence
+request at 1 ms without changing its absolute deadline. Normal BO allocation
+and host import collect completed resources but do not evict aged cache items.
+Successful fence observers claim at most one old item, then release its native
+mapping, lease and storage outside the retirement mutex. A reserved item cannot
+be borrowed or released by another collector. Busy or failed releases publish
+their partial unmap progress while retaining ownership. Memory-pressure and
+allocation-failure flushing remain immediate.
+
+CPU map/unmap use diagnostic phases 31/32, within 33 records per group. A map
+outside a profiled draw/pass creates a MapBuffer group (API 7, command 0); a
+nested map stays in its existing parent group. These wall times include waits
+and scheduling. Host checks cover routing and lifetime; latency needs hardware
+comparison.
+
+
+The initial GMEM tile-store command-stream reservation now returns its Vulkan
+error before entering unchecked packet emission. EndRenderPass frees its
+temporary state and skips later GPU commands; EndCommandBuffer returns the
+existing recording error before appending cache-flush packets. This handles
+the observed null write after a failed BoMap, while preserving the underlying
+mapping failure and032 cache policy. It is not a general GPU reset or a claim
+that every command-stream allocation failure has been handled.
+
+
+Synchronous Metal resource, function and pipeline creation now uses the existing
+compact frame span records (kinds22–29, retained at250us), including nested
+descriptor/runtime/reflection work. Outer calls record Mach THREAD_BASIC_INFO CPU time (1us precision)
+through the previously unused type18 c/d fields, with tid=1 only for a valid
+monotonic CPU delta; older and unmeasured rows retain zero. This distinguishes
+caller work before an encode batch is flushed from work waiting on the encode
+thread. It does not change batching, scheduling, GPU commands or shader caches.
+The optional pipeline journal no longer queries task/thread statistics when it
+is disabled. Hardware latency improvement remains to be measured.
+
+`ndt=1`, with frame diagnostics and a matching native GOP timing endpoint, records raw display counters and hardware timestamps immediately before and after each GPU scanout copy. Format-2 record 26 retains the complete bounded reply in numbered fragments. The source surface handle, kernel object identity and mapping-cache ID are separate fields. Inactive/stale clocks, missing fragments, non-GPU copies and uncertain presentation remain explicit; these records do not turn a software completion into a vblank. GPU034 synchronous API and thread-CPU timing stays enabled for attribution of confirmed long intervals.
+
+
+With display timing enabled, the render server also observes each registered
+CoreAnimation command-creation thread at25/60/110/180ms after the latest
+successful scanout copy. Type27 fragments retain Mach run state, cumulative
+CPU, PC/LR/SP/FP, up to8 frame links, all API errors and observation times.
+Type28 retains current loaded image UUIDs, bases and slides for local decoding.
+The sampler is bounded to8 threads and4096 samples; normal frames perform no
+Mach state query. thread_get_state temporarily stops its target internally;
+stack reads occur after return and matching registers are only a consistency
+check, not an atomic stack guarantee. Observations are joined to independently
+verified physical display intervals and do not themselves measure a slow frame.
+Shared-event waits and encodeDrain now use the existing wall-only span kinds30/31.
+Rendering, deadlines, callbacks, queue policies and synchronization are unchanged.
+
+
+The iPhone24A446 observer records the display server's next-update state and
+Mach display timer fields only for the verified QuartzCore UUID and the original
+IOMFBServer primary layout. AccelServer uses that same primary IOMFBServer
+prefix in this exact image: its unadjusted destructor calls IOMFBServerD2 and
+its update virtual slot is inherited. The reader accepts these two exact
+address points, strips data and callback PAC through their proper keys, and
+still verifies the display and timer callback identities. The state wire
+format and read count are unchanged; other subclasses remain rejected. It only reads memory; repeated fields report consistency, not
+atomicity. The encoding worker joins the registered render-thread observations,
+bounded to6144 samples. SpringBoard's actual main thread uses the same Mach/stack
+reader at20ms for at most ten minutes in its own springboard-waits.trace. Both
+files record kern.bootsessionuuid, mach_timebase_info and image UUID/base/slide; cross-
+process attribution requires the same boot and a common uptime clock.
+
+Per-Vulkan-call Mach CPU profiling is now opt-in with
+NATIVE_METAL_DRIVER_CPU_PROFILE=1. By default the old phase hook is not installed.
+Type14 batch start/end records retain cumulative thread CPU nanoseconds in t3,
+the query wall microseconds in cpu0, and validity bit0 in tid2. These query
+brackets are part of the observation cost; CPU deltas are not GPU duration.
+Type31 reports the actual profile-hook policy. Rendering calls and synchronization
+are unchanged; performance and display scheduling still require device evidence.
+
+Freed runtime-owned buffer backing uses the existing spare cache, with its
+64 MiB buffer budget, ten-second expiry and pressure release. Foreground
+allocations can reuse the original mapping and address object before another
+native allocation. Imported resources retain their owner and release ordering.
+
+Pipeline object collection processes only objects whose last holder was released
+since the preceding collection, using the upstream runtime release queue. It
+retains the exact object keys, destruction order and live shader/layout holds.
+
+The display wait reader accepts only the two verified primary server classes
+and the exact IOMFBDisplay or AppleWirelessDisplay identity in QuartzCore
+76ce2a6e. The latter's original destructor reaches the IOMFBDisplay base without
+adjusting this; the reader accesses no fields of the derived display. Discovery
+checks at most sixteen returned display objects and prefers the exact base
+identity. It retains the selected class in decoded evidence, so an observed
+wireless display is not represented as a physical panel.
+
 Host regression tests cover descriptor-index exhaustion and retirement, completion timeouts, concurrent cache imports, cache ownership checks, heap placement, indirect declarations, texture views and heap retention behind an unsignaled event.
 
 End-to-end macOS 27.3 (`26C5035r`) rendering, live window dragging, zero-copy host-import linear textures and fault-free SMMU/GPU execution have been validated on `sheng` (Adreno 740).
+
+## nezha cumulative GPU088 integration
+
+The runtime-v2 bridge retains the local App Library index and shared AIR reply reuse, foreground allocator sizing, pending-resource wait queries, iOS runtime-thread QoS and Mach running-time clock. IOSurface use belongs to each command until real completion; cached textures retain mappings without holding the backing permanently in use. Native optimal heap images and in-place imports replace the legacy tiled-shadow path; its original implementation and evidence remain on the retained GPU087 branch. Pipeline/lifetime and cache behavior use the v2 ownership and registry paths.
+
+This source integration is not a hardware performance result. The original iPhone-specific layout guards, actual display and UI samplers, resource ownership, real fence/completion boundaries, and all native GPU/CPU frequency and atomic-submit changes stay required in the paired kernel/package.
